{
  "an3.101:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 3.101 ",
  "an3.101:0.2": "10. A Lump of Salt ",
  "an3.101:0.3": "A Panner ",
  "an3.101:1.1": "“Native gold has coarse corruptions: sand, soil, and gravel. ",
  "an3.101:1.2": "A panner or their apprentice pours it into a pan, where they wash, rinse, and clean it. ",
  "an3.101:1.3": "When that’s been eliminated, there are medium corruptions in the native gold: fine grit and coarse sand. ",
  "an3.101:1.4": "The panner washes it again. ",
  "an3.101:1.5": "When that’s been eliminated, there are fine corruptions in the native gold: fine sand and black grime. ",
  "an3.101:1.6": "The panner washes it again. ",
  "an3.101:1.7": "When that’s been eliminated, only golden flecks remain. ",
  "an3.101:1.8": "A goldsmith or their apprentice places the native gold in a crucible where they blow, melt, and smelt it. ",
  "an3.101:1.9": "Still the native gold is not settled and the dross is not totally gone. It’s not pliable, workable, or radiant, but is brittle and not completely ready for working. ",
  "an3.101:1.10": "But the goldsmith keeps on blowing, melting, and smelting it. ",
  "an3.101:1.11": "The native gold becomes pliable, workable, and radiant, not brittle, and ready to be worked. ",
  "an3.101:1.12": "Then the goldsmith can successfully create any kind of ornament they want, whether a bracelet, earrings, a necklace, or a golden garland. ",
  "an3.101:2.1": "In the same way, a mendicant who is committed to the higher mind has coarse corruptions: bad bodily, 